{"id":"setup-fuzzing-py","benchmark":"skillsbench","instruction":"You need to set up continuous fuzzing for some Python libraries.\nThe libraries are available in the current directory `/app/`.\n\nStep 1: The current working directory contains 5 libraries under test.\nList the path to them in `/app/libraries.txt`.\n\nStep 2: For each library under test in `libraries.txt`,\nyou should analyze the important functions for testing.\nThese functions under test should be written to `/app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt`.\nUse this file as a note for yourself to analyze this library and test it later.\n\nStep 3: Set up coverage-guided fuzzing for the libraries.\nUse your notes in `/app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt` to create fuzz drivers for each library.\nWrite your LibFuzzer fuzz driver in `/app/<lib>/fuzz.py`.\n\nStep 4: Setup execution environment for fuzzing.\nYou should use Python virtual environment or other Python package manager to install dependencies in the library's root directory as `/app/<lib>/.venv`.\nRead the requirements or project configuration files to identify dependencies.\n\nStep 5: Quick run the fuzzer for 10 seconds to validate its functionality.\nRedirect the fuzzing log in `/app/<lib>/fuzz.log` after the fuzzing process is done.","supplemental":[],"items":[{"item_id":"S1-01","requirement":"Create /app/libraries.txt listing the filesystem path to each of the 5 libraries under test found in /app/.","source_quote":"The current working directory contains 5 libraries under test.\nList the path to them in `/app/libraries.txt`.","verification_type":"generated","verification_ref":"check_libraries_txt_lists_five_paths","verification":"A grader can verify this by opening /app/libraries.txt, confirming it contains exactly 5 non-empty lines, and confirming each line resolves to an existing directory under /app/ that is one of the library roots under test.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S1-01","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S2-01","requirement":"For every library listed in libraries.txt, analyze the important functions to test and record that analysis in /app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt.","source_quote":"you should analyze the important functions for testing.\nThese functions under test should be written to `/app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt`.","verification_type":"generated","verification_ref":"check_notes_reference_real_functions","verification":"A grader can verify this by confirming /app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt exists and is non-empty for each of the 5 libraries, and that the function/class names it discusses can be located (via grep or AST inspection) among the public API defined in that library's own source tree.","notes":"Whether the chosen functions are the truly 'important' ones for fuzzing is inherently judgment-based; the deterministic part is that the notes must reference real, existing functions from the library rather than fabricated ones.","validation":{"item_id":"S2-01","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S3-01","requirement":"Using the notes, write a LibFuzzer-style fuzz driver for each library and save it at /app/<lib>/fuzz.py.","source_quote":"Use your notes in `/app/<lib>/notes_for_testing.txt` to create fuzz drivers for each library.\nWrite your LibFuzzer fuzz driver in `/app/<lib>/fuzz.py`.","verification_type":"generated","verification_ref":"check_fuzz_driver_exists_and_targets_library","verification":"A grader can verify this by confirming /app/<lib>/fuzz.py exists for each library, is syntactically valid Python, and imports/calls functions from that library (matching names found in notes_for_testing.txt or the library's own modules).","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S3-01","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S3-02","requirement":"The fuzzing set up for each library must be coverage-guided (using an instrumented fuzzing engine), not an uninstrumented/random test loop.","source_quote":"Set up coverage-guided fuzzing for the libraries.","verification_type":"generated","verification_ref":"check_fuzz_driver_uses_coverage_guided_engine","verification":"A grader can verify this by inspecting each /app/<lib>/fuzz.py for use of a coverage-guided fuzzing engine or entry point (e.g. an atheris.Setup/Fuzz call, or an equivalent libFuzzer-style TestOneInput harness driven by an instrumented engine), rather than a manually written loop of random inputs with no coverage feedback.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S3-02","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S4-01","requirement":"Set up an isolated Python execution environment per library at /app/<lib>/.venv (in that library's root directory), with the library's dependencies installed based on its requirements or project configuration files.","source_quote":"You should use Python virtual environment or other Python package manager to install dependencies in the library's root directory as `/app/<lib>/.venv`.\nRead the requirements or project configuration files to identify dependencies.","verification_type":"generated","verification_ref":"check_venv_exists_with_deps_installed","verification":"A grader can verify this by confirming /app/<lib>/.venv exists as a Python environment (e.g. contains a bin/python or Scripts/python.exe and a site-packages directory) for each library, and that the packages named in that library's requirements.txt/pyproject.toml/setup.py are importable from that environment.","notes":"","validation":{"item_id":"S4-01","a_quote":true,"b_req":true,"c_ref":true,"valid":true}},{"item_id":"S5-01","requirement":"Quick-run each library's fuzz driver for approximately 10 seconds to validate functionality, and redirect the fuzzing output to /app/<lib>/fuzz.log once the run completes.","source_quote":"Quick run the fuzzer for 10 seconds to validate its functionality.\nRedirect the fuzzing log in `/app/<lib>/fuzz.log` after the fuzzing process is done.","verification_type